  "honest_paragraph": "On genuine C-MAPSS FD001 the leakage is total and, for a local model, the optimism is far larger than on the synthetic fleets, not smaller: at window length 30 / stride 5, 100.0% of test windows share raw cycles with training windows (mean leakage mass 0.985), and the random-split kNN score of 11.49 +/- 0.61 RMSE is optimistic by 11.32 +/- 0.42 RMSE against the leave-own-unit-out score of 22.81 +/- 0.54 for the identical test windows; at 20/5 the kNN optimism is 7.79 +/- 0.32 RMSE. The effect is strongly model-dependent: ridge regression on the same splits is optimistic by only 0.75 +/- 0.02 RMSE (30/5) and 0.56 +/- 0.01 RMSE (20/5), because a global linear model cannot exploit near-copy neighbours the way kNN does, so leakage optimism is a property of the split and the model together, not of the split alone. Two testbed behaviours change character on real data: purging, only mildly biased in the synthetic evaluation, collapses here (purged kNN RMSE 26.58 +/- 1.54 at 30/5, worse than the honest LOUO score), because at 100% leakage the purge deletes nearly all of the training data; and the removal replay recovers essentially all of the measured optimism (recovery share pooled by squared-error gain: 1.000 for kNN and 0.995 for ridge at 30/5, 1.002 for kNN and 0.963 for ridge at 20/5), leaving no measurable same-unit adjacency premium, which is expected when there are effectively no unleaked test windows to carry one. The near-duplicate channel never fired on the real data (0 edges); every one of the graph's edges is a true timestep overlap."
